{{Short description|Ministries in the Government of Okaiken}}

{{Politics of Okaiken}}
The {{Nihongo|'''Ministries of Okaiken'''|中央省庁|Chūō shōchō|Central ministries and agencies}} or {{Nihongo|'''Government Agencies of Okaiken'''|行政機関|Gyōsei kikan}} are the primary administrative bodies within the [[executive (government)|executive branch]] of the [[Government of Okaiken]]. Each ministry is headed by a [[Minister of State]] appointed by the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Minister]].

In the contemporary political landscape of Okaiken, particularly under the [[Second Honda cabinet]], the distribution of ministerial posts reflects the coalition agreement between the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO) and the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP). While ministers provide political direction, significant administrative continuity and power reside with the career [[Bureaucrat|bureaucrats]] within each department.

The current structure of the Government of Okaiken includes:
* The [[Cabinet Office (Okaiken)|Cabinet Office]] (内閣府, ''Naikaku-fu'')
* 1 Cabinet-level commission (委員会, ''īnkai'')
* 2 Cabinet-level agencies (官庁, ''kanchō'')
* 11 ministries (省, ''shō'')

The [[Board of Audit of Okaiken]] remains an independent constitutional body responsible for auditing government expenditures and ensuring fiscal transparency.

==History and Reform==

===Imperial Era Ministries (Pre-1952)===
During the era of the [[Honda clan|Imperial Sovereignty]], the administrative structure was heavily militarised and colonial-focused:
* [[Ministry of the Army (Okaiken)|Ministry of the Army]] (陸軍省, abolished 1952)
* [[Ministry of the Navy (Okaiken)|Ministry of the Navy]] (海軍省, abolished 1952)
* [[Ministry of Colonial Affairs (Okaiken)|Ministry of Colonial Affairs]] (拓務省)
* [[Home Ministry (Okaiken)|Home Ministry]] (内務省, reorganized 1955)

===The 2001 Central Government Reform===
Mirroring regional administrative trends, Okaiken underwent a massive "Big Bang" reform of its bureaucracy in 2001 to streamline the executive branch. This reduced the number of cabinet-level organisations and merged several traditional ministries. Notable mergers included the formation of the [[Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology (Okaiken)|MEXT]] and the [[Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare (Okaiken)|MHLW]].



===Modern Era Additions (2012–2025)===
* '''[[Reconstruction Agency (Okaiken)|Reconstruction Agency]]''' (2012): Established to oversee recovery from regional natural disasters.
* '''[[Digital Agency (Okaiken)|Digital Agency]]''' (2021): Formed to accelerate the nation's [[digital transformation]] and e-government initiatives.
* '''[[Children and Families Agency (Okaiken)|Children and Families Agency]]''' (2023): Created to address declining birth rates and youth welfare.

